iable when it fails under pressure and with clear evidence. The wrench's failure mode is the easiest to measure in a real mechanical setup: it spins to death."}, {"feedback_id": "FB-V-G09-A-P02-W3", "worker": 3, "valid": false, "parsed": null, "raw_response": "The user wants the tool, not the hands.\n\nSensible. This is the question people have been bein' fussy about, and it is a real bug worth paying attention to. The body-movements and the context-signal all need to be fine-tuned before they move:\n\n1. A wrench seized in the right moment is a different problem than one that just sits there, and the body's own signals need to be processed first.\n2. A seized-tool body is a signal to slow down: resist the urge to finish it, and keep the hands, eyes, and legs in place.\n3. A mutated body is a signal to keep goin' the way you did: stop blaming the"}]}]


